Z||000000||doughboySE||04-24-2000||10:41 PM||||ok here is my question i took the baby in to get rid of the shake, it had nothing to do with ball joints or tie rods, it was as simple as a broken belt in my rear tire and the tire also had a flat spot.<BR>ok back to the question i have new tires on the front bf goodrich <BR>p195/60/14<BR>on the back now i have<BR>p215/60/14<BR>i want to keep at least the 215/60/14 on the back as it seems to be wider.<BR>the question is can i get anything bigger on a stock pontiac rim? or should i stay with the 215 (i love a wide tire ) but i like a narrower one on the front it seems easer to stear at low speeds what you think?<BR>8 am its getting done||||reg||1
Z||000001||GT Bastard||04-24-2000||10:57 PM||||Stock rims are 14 by 6's...  I've got 15 by 6 aftermarket rims, and I'm going to be running 225's all around.  I know another Fiero running the same setup here with no problems. Keep in mind that the 225's on the front will make it slightly more difficult to turn at low speeds, but I doubt it will make a major difference.||||reg||1
Z||000002||doughboySE||04-24-2000||11:06 PM||||GT so is a 215/60/14 the widest i can go on that stock rim?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Patrick||04-25-2000||12:57 AM||||<BR>Ideally, I believe the width of the tire tread is supposed to be the same as the width of the rim, but you could probably put 235/60/14 tires on those rims. Just make sure that you have clearance on the <I>inside</I> of the tires in the back. I'm referring to the clearance between the tires and the rear suspension.<p>[This message has been edited by Patrick (edited 04-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||04-25-2000||01:20 AM||||The biggest tire you can put on a 14" X 6" rim is 236/60R14. I have BFGoodrich T/A's at all four corners. I have not had and clearence problems.<BR>OOPS I meant 235/60R14. Tried to correct during mid-post and now I see I have a double post.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 04-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||04-25-2000||01:21 AM||||The biggest tire you can put on a 14" X 6" rim is 235/60R14. I have BFGoodrich T/A's at all four corners. I have not had and clearence problems.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Patrick||04-25-2000||03:41 AM||||<BR>Way to screw up the thread, Oreif. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Yeah, I just came across some Goodyear Tire paperwork I had and it states that 235/60/14 tires will fit on rims from 6 to 9 inches wide. The 245/60/14 tires require wheels at least 6.5 inches wide.<P>My concern with clearances is that different offsets of wheels will of course fit the same car differently. It might be a mistake to assume that all <I>stock</I> Fiero wheels have the same offset. I don't know, I'm just wondering.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||87PegasusGT||04-25-2000||03:34 PM||||If that is correct, I should be able to put 245/50/15 on my 87 GT wheels. Correct? They should be 15 x 7.||||reg||1
Z||000008||doughboySE||04-25-2000||07:13 PM||||THANX TO ALL I HAVE SOLVED THE PROBLEM!!!!1<BR>i went with what i had there 215/60/14<BR>they look good and are larger then the front which is the way i wanted it<BR>took her out for a test drive to see if i lost the shake and wooooooooooooow finally no shake even at 160kms /h i didnt even need to steer the car <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>only did that for a min though dont need a ticket<BR>thanx again everyone<BR>GOOOOOOOOOO LEAFS||||reg||1
Z||000009||Monkeyman||04-26-2000||09:52 AM||||100mph (for those that can't do the conversion) is a little fast up there.  I got caught going about that fast the 1st time I was in the Great White North.  (I was in a truck.)  I saw the sign (as I crossed the bridge) that said 100kph=62mph but misread it.  I didn't notice the <B>k</B> as opposed to the <B>m</B>.  I guess I thought that they meant that our average speed limit was 62mph and yours was 100<B>m</B>ph.  Oops!  The OPP officer was mighty friendly considering he could have jailed me and impounded my truck.||||reg||1
